// A request message of the GetDataUsage method.
//
// GetDataUsage reads the data usage of one SIM directly from the carrier at the
// time of the call and sums it per APN and period, or, with the aggregation
// period NONE, returns the carrier's records one by one. Use it to check the
// usage that the Monitoring service serves as metrics, and to troubleshoot with
// what the carrier reports right now. For regular use of usage data, query the
// Monitoring service metrics instead: they are collected continuously, are not
// bound by the carrier's retention, and do not put a request on the carrier for
// every query.
//
// Conditions to be aware of:
//
// - The data comes directly from the carrier. Every call is a request to the
//   carrier, admitted by the request-rate budget that the telemetry collection
//   of the same carrier account shares. The call waits until the budget admits
//   it. While a collection cycle is running, or after the carrier answered with
//   a rate-limit error (which holds back all requests for a few seconds), it
//   can take longer than a client's default timeout, such as 10 seconds. Set a
//   timeout of at least 60 seconds, or retry on DEADLINE_EXCEEDED.
// - Old usage may not be available. The carrier keeps usage records only for a
//   limited time, so a window older than its retention returns no points. That
//   is not the same as no usage. The window is also limited to 31 days. For
//   Transatel, the retention of the usage records (raw CDRs) is not published.
//   In a measurement of October 2026, the records of the current UTC day and
//   of the 89 days before it were available, and older days were empty. That is
//   an observation, not a guarantee, so do not rely on usage older than about
//   85 days.
// - Time ranges. A carrier record is stamped with one event time. The carrier
//   does not document which period of a session its usage covers, so the whole
//   usage of a record counts in the UTC hour of its event time. start_time is
//   inclusive and end_time exclusive, and they need not be aligned. Each point
//   covers one UTC hour, day or month, cut by the window at its ends. A whole
//   HOURLY point has the same interval as the matching point of the Monitoring
//   metric data_usage, which is written per accounting hour. A Monitoring query
//   with a 3600s alignment period labels that hour with its end time: the
//   hour [00:00, 01:00) is the point at 01:00. With NONE, a point is one record
//   and its start_time and end_time are the time of the record.
// - Usage is reported with a delay. The carrier reports a data session some
//   time after it happens, so the most recent hours can be incomplete and grow
//   when more records appear later. A record that has appeared does not
//   change. The Monitoring metrics have the same delay: a record is added at
//   the first collection after the carrier exposes it. Compare the two only
//   for hours that are well in the past, and see as_of in the response for when
//   the carrier was read.
